Body:
Confirming this same root cause manifests at a different surface — long-lived stdio MCP servers, not just `bash`-tool-spawned background children.

**Reproducer** (minimal): in `opencode.json`, configure any stdio MCP that keeps its process alive past tool-call return. We hit it with `@modelcontextprotocol/server-everything`:

```json
{
  "mcp_servers": {
    "everything": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-everything"]
    }
  }
}
```

Run a normal task that invokes one of the MCP's tools (e.g. `everything_echo`). The tool call succeeds and the agent prints its summary — but the parent opencode process never exits, because `cross-spawn-spawner.ts`'s `close` listener still has open inherited stdio pipes from the npx → server-everything child.

Same diagnosis as the original bug body — `close` waits for stdio descriptors to release; the daemon keeps them alive forever; `close` never fires.

**Symptom in our setup:** opencode runs to logical completion (final commit pushed, message printed) at +137s. State stays "running" indefinitely; we observed 7+ minutes before manually killing. The MCP wiring itself is fine — tool calls, prompts, schema all work.

I see [@earlzero](https://github.com/earlzero) tried the `exit`-event fix and found forked processes get killed at 2 min. For the MCP-cleanup case specifically, an explicit `MCPServer.close()` (per the [MCP spec's transport teardown](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/basic/transports#shutdown)) on session end might be a more targeted fix than swapping the spawner event — closing the JSON-RPC channel should signal the server to exit normally, releasing its stdio.

Tracking from our side at [mcdays94/twelve-angry-agents#9](https://github.com/mcdays94/twelve-angry-agents/issues/9) for anyone wanting to follow the MCP angle. Happy to test any candidate fix against our reproducer.

I've been having the same issue on Linux/macOS for a while now. The configuration below will allow to write in the `frontend` directory instead of denying it.

Wondering why the `bug` tag was removed.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "permission": {
    "external_directory": {
      "/home/roan/development/projects/app/frontend/**": "allow",
    },
    "edit": {
      "/home/roan/development/projects/app/frontend/**": "deny"
    }
  }
}
```

@thdxr You are working on the new event system, right?

As  https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent/issues/3816 rightfully points out plugins should not be allowed to change/modify arguments like tool calls to prevent them from braking out. However, as the current v1 event system (probably unconsciously) established, plugins rely on / make use of receiving mutable arguments as a plugin system "feature". 

What are your plans for the new v2 event system? Should plugins receive a mutable reference of the arguments, or a copy? How should plugins return a modification of arguments?
